{
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "composite": false,
    "declaration": false,
    "declarationMap": false,
    "noEmit": true,
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "types": []
  },
  // `perf` joins `src` because it is APPLICATION code — Vite builds it
  // (`vite.perf.config.ts`), and Vite does not typecheck, so a type error there would
  // surface only as a broken perf run.
  //
  // `e2e-perf` was the first carve-out (QC round-1 fix 10): `stress.perf.spec.ts` hand-
  // maintains a `declare global { interface Window { wyndingPerf?: … } }` block that
  // must structurally match `perf/main-perf.ts`'s own `PerfHarness` interface (see that
  // file's header for why it is a hand-copy rather than a shared import). Nothing
  // enforced the two staying in sync — including `e2e-perf` here means `tsc` catches a
  // drift as a type error instead of a spec silently reading `undefined` off a renamed
  // or removed field. Pulling `@playwright/test`'s types in for this one directory did
  // not need any special handling: `stress.perf.spec.ts` imports `test`/`expect`
  // explicitly, so its types resolve the same way any other npm package's would.
  //
  // `e2e` (#110 — supersedes the earlier "stays out, matching this repo's existing
  // convention" note that used to live here): the old rationale was about dependency
  // direction ("nothing outside them depends on their types"), which is true but beside
  // the point — these specs are CI's whole functional-UI gate (the a11y e2e job), so a
  // type error inside one should fail loud as a type error, not surface later as an
  // assertion that silently stops asserting. Including it added no edges (nothing
  // outside `e2e/**` imports from it), and the fallout was one call site: `stage-
  // stability.spec.ts`'s `page.evaluate(([x, y]) => …, [down.x, down.y])` inferred `x`/
  // `y` as `number | undefined` (array-literal argument, not a tuple) — rewritten as the
  // `{ x, y }` object-argument form, which `layout-probe.ts` already uses for the same
  // reason. (Most `page.evaluate` calls in this directory take no argument or a single
  // scalar and were unaffected.)
  "include": ["src", "perf", "e2e-perf", "e2e"],
  "references": [
    { "path": "../../packages/render" },
    { "path": "../../packages/sim" },
    { "path": "../../packages/content" },
    { "path": "../../packages/engine" },
    { "path": "../../packages/replay" },
    { "path": "../../packages/perf" }
  ]
}
